{"name":"Copper Moon","hex":"#c29978","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"copper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A reddish-brown metallic chemical element (symbol Cu) with the atomic number 29; also, the metal made up of this element.","The reddish-brown colour of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1).","Having the reddish-brown colour of copper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/copper"},{"term":"moon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Moon (“the Earth's only permanent natural satellite”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any natural satellite of a planet.","The light received from the Moon; moonlight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Copper","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/330px-NatCopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper","wikidata":"Q753"},{"title":"Moon","blocks":[{"text":"The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits Earth in an elliptical path, at a distance that varies over the year, averaging around 378,000 kilometers (235,000 mi). It completes an orbit in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period to its orbital period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/330px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon","wikidata":"Q405"},{"title":"Copper (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a reddish brown color that resembles the metal copper. The first recorded use of copper as a color name in English was in 1594."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Copper-18706.jpg/330px-Copper-18706.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_(color)","wikidata":"Q2722041"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/500px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/1280px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Cu-Scheibe.JPG/500px-Cu-Scheibe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A copper disc (99.95% pure) made by continuous casting; etched to reveal crystallites","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACu-Scheibe.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif/250px-Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Molten_copper_in_bright_sunlight.gif?utm_source=en.wi